ng for a tactical defense.* It looks like the most pressing threat is the horizontal line on Row 7: I7, J7, K7. *Oh my, I really need to block that*. If I don\'t respond *immediately*, I\'ll be in a world of trouble. This is my priority.\n\nLet\'s investigate Black\'s threat, "straight 3", I7-J7-K7 on Row 7. Black could make it "Open 4" if he can play at the open ends (H7 or L7).\n\nSo, now it is my turn to figure out where to play to block this threat. *Let\'s see: I have to block Black\'s threat*. To block, I must play H7 or L7.\n\nI will explore the options (H7 and L7). *Now it\'s time to choose, but this is a critical choice*. Blocking at H7: Blocks Black\'s threat. Forms White "diagonal 3". Blocking at L7: Blocks Black\'s threat but doesn\'t create anything.\n\nPlaying at H7, creates a diagonal 3 for me, creating an instant threat that forces Black to react. If I make a diagonal of H7, I8, J9, my "3" is a valid threat. If White plays H7, Black must block the White 3.\n\nI\'m checking that threat to confirm (8,7)(9,8)(10,9). Everything is there. I see an "Open 3" for White, if I play H7! If I play H7, Black has to respond. *This move could give me initiative.*\n\nI will evaluate my other option, L7. Blocking, but no follow-up.\n\nI\'ll choose H7: Block Black\'s 3, and immediately creating a counter-threat. *That\'s a good move, I think.* Playing at L7, is purely defensive, and I want to be offensive, because there is no other immediate win in sight.\n\nThen I am considering my options. Play H7, and build H7-I8-J9.\n\nIf I play H7 (defending) Black cannot win immediately, but if Black can get the "Open Four" in an unchecked move, I lose.\n\nNow I am re-assessing for any sneaky mistakes. *Maybe there is something else!* Then I remember I must block, and H7 looks like the best option. I need to make the correct move.\n\nI carefully confirm the stones.\n\n*Double Check*. It does block Black\'s immediate threat, and sets me up for a follow-up.\n\nThe position could be bad. Does this lead to a loss?\n\n*Board is re-checked*. H7 is the move. I analyze. I check the diagonal and the line for the "four" (it becomes an "open four"). \nThis could be\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board position is critical for White. Black has established a "Double Three" threat:\n1.  **Horizontal:** Stones at **I7, J7, K7** form a "Live 3" (open at both ends, H7 and L7). If not blocked, Black can play H7 or L7 to form an "Open 4", which is an unstoppable win.\n2.  **Diagonal:** Stones at **I5, J6, K7** form another "Live 3" (open at H4 and L8).\n\nWhite is in a dangerous position and must block to survive. White cannot passively defend; a move must be chosen that blocks a threat and simultaneously creates counter-play.\n\n**Analysis of the move H7:**\n*   **Defense:** Placing a stone at **H7** directly blocks Black\'s horizontal threat (I7-J7-K7).\n*   **Offense:** The move at H7 aligns with White\'s existing stones at **I8** and **J9** to form a diagonal "Live 3" (H7-I8-J9). This creates an immediate threat for White (threatening to extend to G6 or K10 to make 4).\n\nWhile Black\'s diagonal threat (I5-J6-K7) remains dangerous, blocking at H7 is the best tactical choice because it seizes the initiative (Sente) by forcing Black to consider White\'s new threat, whereas other blocks (like L7, H4, or L8) would be purely passive and lead to a quicker loss.\n\nReason: White must block Black\'s immediate horizontal threat on row 7. Playing at H7 is the optimal defensive move because it simultaneously forms a diagonal 3-in-a-row (H7-I8-J9) for White, generating a counter-threat that complicates Black\'s attack.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 7}'}
