eat. I must prevent that to prevent a loss. L8 is adjacent to more of my stones, so this might make it a more effective block to play. Therefore, the only viable move is to block Black's immediate threat by placing a White stone at L8. I double-check again. Yes, it is the only move. I make a note about the board state and the move, as I deliver my answer.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: Black has a lethal threat with an Open 4 on the diagonal line H4, I5, J6, K7. This formation is unblocked at both ends (G3 and L8), meaning Black can win on the next turn by playing either spot. White must block immediately to prolong the game. White also has a strong potential diagonal line (H7-I8-J9), but attacking is not an option when facing an immediate loss.\n\nReason: The diagonal consisting of Black stones at H4, I5, J6, and K7 forms an Open 4. If White does not block one of the ends (G3 or L8), Black will play there to form 5-in-a-row and win. Blocking at L8 is chosen as it is closer to the center of the board and White\'s other stones, potentially offering more defensive resources than the remote G3.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "L", "row": 8}'}
